WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.800
This camera, part of a flying laboratory, is recovered by Clyde Holliday of Johns Hopkins

00:06.800 --> 00:09.960
who prepared it for its trip to the upper stratosphere.

00:09.960 --> 00:14.680
Rivaling the fantastic imagination of Jules Verne, the camera brought back a record of

00:14.680 --> 00:19.180
a flight into the heavens of a captured German B-2 rocket.

00:19.180 --> 00:27.120
At White Sands, New Mexico, the huge missile takes off.

00:27.120 --> 00:31.480
Air force pictures show the rocket in flight, and the flying camera automatically takes

00:31.480 --> 00:32.480
over.

00:32.480 --> 00:37.480
The huge projectile drops the Earth behind at the tremendous speed of 4,000 feet per

00:37.480 --> 00:38.720
second.

00:38.720 --> 00:43.440
The rotation of the rocket causes the planet to spin before the lens, and the camera photographs

00:43.440 --> 00:47.080
the Earth 65 miles straight down.

00:47.080 --> 00:52.840
The horizon, 720 miles away, and the curvature of the Earth are astonishingly apparent in

00:52.840 --> 00:55.280
this still picture from the film.

00:55.280 --> 00:59.520
An observer in the rocket could have seen San Diego, Salt Lake City, Kansas City, and

00:59.520 --> 01:01.120
San Antonio.

01:01.120 --> 01:06.680
Approximately 1,600,000 square miles of the Earth's surface was revealed.

01:06.680 --> 01:10.320
The rocket reached the 65-mile height in three minutes.

01:10.320 --> 01:15.260
This giant engine of destruction, designed by Hitler to annihilate allied nations, now

01:15.260 --> 01:26.840
serves the worthy cause of peacetime research.

